## 1. Introduction
Carboxylic acid is one of the most important organic compounds in daily human life activities such as in food preservatives, pesticides, dyes intermediates, coatings, plasticizers, spices, and pharmaceuticals.[1] Developing efficient and sustainable synthetic processes for the preparation of carboxylic acids has attracted great attentions from many scholars both industry and academia. The most efficient method for its preparation is the direct oxidation of primary alcohols in the chemical process. However, this method of chemical transformation was traditionally carried out using harsh reaction conditions like super stoichiometric, toxic, and economically intolerant oxidants such as ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ , $\mathsf{K M n O}_{4}$ $\mathsf{K}_{2}\mathsf{C r}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{7}.$ , etc. as indicated in Scheme 1a, [2–5] which would lead to a series of severe environmental problems because of the excess release of chemical waste. Hence, in order to overcome such environmental and economic problems, the application of traditional oxidants such as molecular $0_{2}$ , as an abundant and green sustainable natural feedstock as found to be a highly efficient method for oxidation of alcohols to corresponding carboxylic acids.[6–12].
Recently, thermo-catalytic methods have been developed for the oxidation of alcohols with molecular oxygen as shown in Scheme 1b. It was also found that the application of metal–ligand complex homogeneous catalysts [9,13], heterogeneous catalysts [14–22], and base-based catalysts [21,23,24] with molecular oxygen have efficiently transformed alcohols to corresponding carboxylic acids. Additionally, photocatalytic oxidation of alcohols has been disclosed by using Br- source or commercial organic dyes as is clearly shown in Scheme 1c, [25–27] where activation of Brneeds UV irradiation that results in emissions of environmental pollutant organic dyes. Nowadays, many researchers have made important progress in designing multi-component catalysts that exploit the catalytic activities of different components to achieve high-yield and selective alcohol-to-acid oxidation[28–30]. For example, Stahl et.al. have disclosed a TEMPO-based co-catalytic system in the aerobic oxidation reaction of alcohols under the application of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H_{2}O}/\mathrm{TEMPO}/\mathrm{KCl}.[31$ ].
Unlike the above methods, Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) have gained great attention for decades owing to inherent large surface areas, uniform but tunable cavities, and modifiable structure.[32–36] In recent times, many researchers have focused on the designing, synthesis, and applications of MOFs in catalyzing chemical transformations. $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti), is one of the efficient photo-catalyst that have noble photocatalytic applications in sunlight-driven organic transformations.[37–39] Even though, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) and its modified photocatalytic materials have been successfully employed to achieve the selective catalytic oxidation of alcohols and amines to corresponding aldehydes and imines, [36,40,41] they have their drawbacks like a poor transformation of substrates especially aliphatic alcohols and amines and hence their application is restricted in both academic and industry. However, the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) catalyst remained large potential in the synthesis of carboxylic acids from alcohols but there existed a fundamental challenge. To design an efficient catalyst system, the reaction system should contain redox-active components such as halogen (Br-, Cl- and I-), and $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ to enhance electrons transformation in oxidative reactions,[30] which is a good ‘‘bridge” that connects oxidants and substrates. To the best of our knowledge, bromide $(\mathsf{B r^{-}})$ has been used in the photocatalytic oxidative reaction under UV irradiation,[27,42] in combination with $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) to design an efficient photo-catalyst system which has potential application in the visible-light-driven oxidative transformations.
In the current work, sodium bromide (NaBr) has been used as a co-catalyst to increase catalytic activity and efficiency for propelling the aerobic oxidation of alcohols under visible light irradiation, which is environmentally friendly and efficient in the transformation of alcohols to the corresponding carboxylic acids as shown in Scheme 1d.
