## 2.2. Catalyst preparation
Preparation of $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti): $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) MOF catalyst was prepared following the literature.[36] Accordingly, 2- aminoterephthalic acid $\left({\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{A T A}}\right)$ (2.178 g, 12 mmol), $18~\mathrm{mL}$ of N, $N_{\l}$ -Dimethylformamide (DMF), and $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol (MeOH) were added to a Teflon liner with a magneton, and $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of Titanium nbutoxide $\mathrm{(Ti(OC_{4}H_{9})_{4}},$ was added dropwise. Then, the above mixture was stirred for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the magneton was removed. Finally, the whole liner was packed in stainless steel autoclave and heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The reactor was cooled to room temperature to separate the solid and washed with DMF and methanol, respectively, and the yellow solid was obtained $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)).
Preparation of MIL-125 (Ti): MIL-125 (Ti) was prepared by using the same procedure as that for $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti), except using terephthalic acid instead of 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{A T A})$ which was added into the mixture during hydrothermal process.
## 2.3. Electrochemical characterization
All the electrochemical measurements were performed on a Chenhua instruments electrochemical station by using a traditional three-electrode quartz cell with a $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ aqueous $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S O}_{4}$ solution as electrolyte. The solution of $2{\mathrm{~mg}}$ catalyst in $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol was sonicated into a slurry and dripped onto clean FTO glass. The dry catalyst-coated FTO (exposed area $2\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times\ 2\ \mathrm{cm}$ ) acted as the working electrode, a Pt plate as the counter electrode, and $\textstyle\mathtt{A g}/\mathtt{A g C l}$ as the reference electrode. The photocurrent signals were measured using the same light source as that for photocatalytic experiments described above $(450\mathrm{nm})$ . Mott-Schottky plots of photocatalyst were measured on an electrochemical workstation at frequencies of 500 and $1500~\mathrm{Hz}$ , respectively.
## 2.4. Catalytic reaction
In a typical procedure, the solution of $5~\mathrm{mg}$ prepared $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL125 (Ti) photocatalyst and $6~\mathrm{mg}$ NaBr (0.3 equiv., $0.06\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ) was added into $8\mathrm{mL}$ ethyl acetate containing $0.2\:\mathrm{mmol}$ of specific alcohols in a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ reaction tube under constant stirring. The molecular $0_{2}$ , which is stored in a balloon, was connected to the reaction tube via a straight suction joint with a grinding port. The reaction unit was placed and irradiated with a single-wavelength light reaction module $(450-630\mathrm{nm})$ equipped with condensation and agitation equipment. After reacting for a certain time, the filtered reaction solution was measured by a gas chromatograph and compared the retention time with the commercial samples. Finally, the desired products were confirmed by $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR.
## 3. Result and discussion
X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) was consistent with the literature as shown in Fig. 1a, [43] transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and scanning electron microscope (SEM) displayed that $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) have formed a regular crystal structure and rule shape (Fig. 2). The UV/Vis absorption of the catalyst was observed in the wavelength band of $400{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating its potential application for the oxidation of alcohols under the range of visible light in the electromagnetic spectrum. Furthermore, the band gap $\left(\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}\right)$ values of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) were calculated and found to be 2.41 eV (Fig. 1b). According to the MottSchottky plots at different frequencies (Fig. 3a), the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) is located at $-0.83\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ (i.e. $-0.63\mathrm{~V~}$ vs normal hydrogen electrode (NHE)). It is known that the conduction band of such an $n$ -type semiconductor is more negative by about $0.10\mathrm{~V~}$ than the flat band potential [44,45]. Thus, the conduction band (LUMO, $\mathbf{E_{CB}},$ of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) can be estimated to be 0.73 V vs NHE. In addition, the valence band (HOMO, EVB) was calculated and found to be $+\ensuremath{1.68\mathrm{V}}$ vs NHE. $'\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{VB}}-\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}})$ . As it is shown in Fig. 3b, photocurrent transient response over several $\mathsf{o n}/$ off cycles of intermittent irradiation was observed to have good reproducibility.
To get the optimization of reaction conditions, benzyl alcohol (1a) was used as a model substrate to determine the ability of the photo-catalyst towards selective oxidation of alcohols using molecular oxygen (Table 1). The conversion of raw material benzyl alcohol was not successful to the desired product either absence of MOFs catalyst or the presence of the catalyst in dark conditions (Table 1, Entry 12, 14). Furthermore, using only $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) without additive led to a very low yield of the mid product, 2a (Table 1, Entry 3). The irradiation wavelength $(450\mathrm{nm})$ ) was chosen because only $450\mathrm{nm}$ irradiation wavelength exhibited perfect catalytic activity (Table 1, Entry 1, 4–6). Other solvents were used instead of the ethyl acetate, however, the conversion and yield decreased dramatically (Table 1, Entry 7–9). Alongside, various additives were used in order to get desired products with high yields. Potassium bromide (KBr) was one of the additives used and afforded the target component (Br-), but the reactivity was lower than that of NaBr cocatalyst in the optimized conditions (Table 1, Entry 1 vs Entry 10). In addition to the above additives, lithium bromide (LiBr) was used instead of sodium bromide (NaBr) and it afforded the corresponding carboxylic acid 3a (Table S1, Entry 1, 5) efficiently. Based on our findings, the KBr co-catalyst exhibited very poor catalytic performance compared with NaBr and LiBr, which might be due to the potassium (K) atom having high steric hindrance than Na or Li atoms that has less atomic size. Hence, smaller LiBr and NaBr were easier to enter the interior of the MOFs and function.[46,47] Moreover, $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S O}_{4}$ was also used as an additive instead of NaBr and afforded a trace amount of the product (Entry 11). In addition to the above additives, various salts of sodium such as NaF, NaCl, and NaI were used. However, the order of increasing conversion of oxidation reaction was found to be $\mathrm{NaBr}>\mathrm{NaCl}>\mathrm{NaF}>\mathrm{NaI}$ (Table S1, Entry 1–4). This might be probably due to the oxidation of halide series is related to electron affinity and formal $\mathrm{E^{\circ}(X^{\bullet}/X^{-})}$ reduction potentials followed the trend $\mathrm{I}<\mathrm{Br}\ll\mathrm{Cl}$ , so I anion is hence the most easily oxidized. [48] Hence, the $\mathsf{F}^{-}$ of NaF was difficult to oxidize into F∙ because of its highest electron affinity. However, NaI is easily oxidized by air and deteriorated, and we found that the reaction solution in the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/NaI system is brown because of the oxidation of NaI to $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ .
It is worth noting that adding KBr gave a better conversion than none of the Br- sources, and we speculated that Br- is the important component of the reaction. Moreover, conversion of benzyl alcohol to the corresponding acid was also observed in the presence of air (Table 1, Entry 16), but a lower benzoic acid conversion was obtained in the atmospheric oxygen compared to pure molecular oxygen under the same reaction conditions. However, the transformation could not realize in an $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, indicating the crucial role of the terminal oxidant, $0_{2}$ (Table 1, Entry 13). In addition, thermos-catalytic had poor reactivity relative to light irradiation (Table 1, Entry 15). In addition to $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)/ NaBr, MIL-125/NaBr system can also catalyze benzyl alcohol to benzoic acid, however, MIL-125 showed no conversion under standard conditions due to its wide band gap $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{Eg}=3.7\mathrm{eV},$ ) as shown in Table S1, Entry 7 and 8. [40] These results indicate that the whole $(\mathsf{N H}_{2^{-}})$ MIL-125 (Ti)/NaBr catalytic system is suitable for the oxidation of alcohols under molecular oxygen to carboxylic acid at $450~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light irradiation.
C balance of the product was calculated based on the standard formula as C balance $(\%)=\sum$ (Number of carbon atoms in product ${\bf j}\times{\bf C}_{\bf j},$ , final) / (Number of carbon atoms in $\mathrm{i}\times(\mathsf C_{\mathrm{i},0^{-}}\mathsf C_{\mathrm{i},\mathsf{f n a l}}))\times100\%,$ $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{i},0}$ was the initial concentration of the substrate (benzyl alcohol, $25\ \mathrm{mmol/L}\rangle$ . $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{i},}$ $\mathrm{\final}$ was the final concentration of the substrate (benzyl alcohol). $C_{\mathrm{j,}}$ final was the final concentration of product (benzaldehyde, benzoic acid and benzoic anhydride).
Under the optimized conditions, we further examined the substrate scope of our catalytic system and hence a wide array of benzyl alcohol derivatives with various substituent groups were selectively oxidized, affording corresponding carboxylic acids in excellent yields (Table 2, Entry 1-9). First, benzyl alcohol with substituent groups on phenyl were tested. For example, the oxidative conversion of bromine (1b) and electron-donating groups, such as methyl (1c) and methoxy (1d) substituted substrates, afforded the corresponding acid products with high yields above $92\%$ However, benzyl alcohol with an electron-withdrawing group is more difficult than an electron-donating group under this catalytic condition for oxidation to the corresponding acids. This was because of the electron effect of the substituents. For instance, the yields of acids derived from the oxidative transformation of chlorine (1e-1g) substituted substrates were relatively lower even in a long reaction time, but still satisfactory. This result solidifies that an alcohol with an electron-withdrawing substituent on the phenyl ring exhibited a decreased acid conversion but electron-donating substituted benzyl alcohol exhibited a good conversion rate with high yields of the product which is consistent with previously reported literature.[36] In addition, the stereo resistance of the aromatic alcohols did not deteriorate the reactivity (1e-1g). The oxidation conversion of aliphatic primary alcohols is another class of important reactions to produce aliphatic carbonyl acids for a wide range of industrial and pharmaceutical applications including biologically active capsaicinoids.[49,50] In general, aromatic alcohols were more reactive than aliphatic alcohols, but aliphatic primary alcohols afforded the product carbonyl acid in high conversion and yield. For instance, long chain linear aliphatic primary alcohols afforded the corresponding carbonyl acid products with yields above $90\%$ (Entries 8 and 9, 1h and 1i). Based on our findings, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)/ NaBr catalytic system possesses superior oxidation photocatalytic activities toward both aromatic alcohols and aliphatic primary alcohols.
It is very meaningful to trace the reaction process of alcohol for photocatalytic mechanism understanding. Based on timedependent transformation from benzyl alcohol (Fig. 4), benzaldehyde was obtained as a product in the first step under the application of molecular $0_{2}$ as oxidant and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) as photocatalyst under visible light irradiation.[41] In this work, NaBr promoted the activity and oxidation of the photocatalysis system of the MOF $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) and makes it more efficient for further transformation of benzyl alcohol to benzoic acid.
To better understand the photocatalytic mechanism of the reaction, the radical scavenger TEMPO (2,2,6,6-Tetramethylpiperidine 1-oxide) was used in the optimized reaction system to confirm the presence of reactive free radical species.[51] As shown in Fig. 5, the transformation of benzyl alcohol (1a) to benzoic acid (3a) was significantly inhibited in which the mid product (benzaldehyde, 2a) remained unreacted following the addition of 2 equiv. TEMPO. In contrast, the transformation proceeded smoothly in the optimized reaction system without TEMPO. Hence, these control experiments indicate that the alcohol oxidation probably proceeded via a radical-mediated pathway. Based on our findings and the previously reported mechanisms[40,52,53], the transfer of photoexcited electrons to reaction species was further proposed as the following pathway (Scheme 2): Given the more positive potential of the VB (HOMO) $_{1.68\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs NHE) in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti), it is thermodynamically feasible for an electron transferred from Br- $\left(\mathrm{E^{\circ}}(\mathrm{Br^{\bullet}}/\mathrm{Br^{-}})=1.92\mathrm{\V}\right.$ vs NHE, $\mathrm{E^{\circ}(B r_{2}^{\bullet-}/2B r^{-})=1.62\ V}$ vs NHE) [48,54–56] to the Ti-O oxo-clusters (LMCT) to form $\mathsf{B r}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ instead of $\mathbf{Br^{\bullet}}$ in one step[57] as shown in Scheme 3.[58] The reaction mechanism is the same as that of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ can react with molecular oxygen to generate related reactive oxygen species (ROS, $\operatorname{E}(0_{2}/$ $0_{2}^{\bullet-})=-0.33\vee$ vs NHE).[56] Specifically, $\mathsf{B r}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ is responsible for alcohol oxidation to form intermediate $\mathrm{PhCH_{2}O H^{\bullet+}}$ (2a’). Accordingly, the formed $\mathsf{B r}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ was reduced back to Br- and $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ was oxidized back to $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ in the reductive quenching cycle through a single electron transfer (SET) process.[58] The superoxide radical can grab a hydrogen atom from $2\mathsf{a}^{\prime}$ to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and 2a. Subsequently, the aldehyde was further oxidized into the corresponding carboxylic acid through the catalytic system. In addition, the stability of the catalyst has been provided as shown in Figure S2 and the conversion decreased after the first cycle, which might be due to the collapse of the partial MOFs under the strong oxidative condition as shown in Figure S3. In this work, the photocatalysis system promotes the photoactivity and oxidation capacity of both the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) and NaBr and realized more convenient to be easily separated from the reaction solution.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have developed a binary $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/ NaBr catalyst system consisting of a photo-catalyst and a redoxactive co-catalyst that is responsible for oxidation of alcohol to the corresponding carboxylic acids. The whole $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)/ NaBr system was found to be a promising method compared to the single one of them for the oxidation of alcohols. Moreover, the catalytic system was also applicable to the broad substrate scope including aliphatic and aromatic alcohols, producing corresponding carboxylic acids. These radical inhibition results indicate that the photocatalytic reaction involved radical-mediated oxidation steps. The photoinduced single electrons transfer (SET) occurs from $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Br^{-}}}$ to $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti), which achieved photogeneration of the strong oxidant bromine radical species and ultimately improves the photocatalytic activity for oxidative alcohols to acids. According to the catalysis system potential analysis, the oxidation of alcohol substrates into carbonyl compounds proceeded via a thermodynamically less demanding pathway under visible light irradiation. Generally, this work provided designing and preparation of an environmentally friendly, economical, and effective photocatalytic system for selective and sensitive oxidation of alcohols to corresponding carboxylic acids.
